{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>The crown of the wise is their wealth; the folly of fools is folly (Proverbs 14:24).</b>" "<b>Solomon</b> the king informs us in this verse (Proverbs 14) that wealth is a quality which the wise attain and in which transgressors stumble. For when wealth is in the possession of the wise, it is to him a crown and glory, because as long as he lacks wealth his wisdom is despised and his words are not heard, but with wealth he has the power to lift up the head of truth and to humble falsehood and all who speak it, and furthermore he may distribute it in virtues and in deeds through which he is crowned and honored. And the verse teaches you that this wealth which is a crown for the wise is, for fools, folly, because fools pride themselves in their wealth and through it they raise a horn to support the hands of all transgressors, and their hand prevails to do every act of wantonness." "<b>And so,</b> wealth is like the sun, which performs two actions opposite to each other: it whitens the garment and blackens the face of the washerman, and all depends on the recipient. So too wealth in its matter contains two actions opposite to each other — a crown for one, folly for another — and all depends on a person's understanding. And the duplication of expression, in that he mentioned אולת twice, comes to say that wealth is folly for the souls of fools. And the upshot of the matter is that the wealth of fools will cause them to stumble and will destroy them with destruction of the body and destruction of the soul, but the wise are careful not to be harmed by it; rather, they are honored through it and derive from it benefit for body and soul, because they know that wealth and success in this world are nothing but preparations for the needs of the soul, so that it may learn wisdom and receive intelligibilia. And from this he mentioned further (Proverbs 15), אולת שמחה לחסר לב ואיש תבונה יישר לכת — for joy and an abundance of tranquility is folly for one lacking a heart: from it there will come to him that he commits folly; but a man of understanding — from it there will come to him that he rectifies his traits through the cause of joy and tranquility; this is יישר לכת — he rectifies through it the traits of his body and his soul. Therefore the Torah always elaborates regarding the performance of the commandments with promises of bodily success and an abundance of tranquility and goodness in this world." "<b>And it is known</b> that those things are not the ultimate end, for they are bodily promises, all of them suited to serve as preparations for the rational soul to seek and attain knowledge of HaShem, in order to merit in the end the light of the face of the King of life, which is the ultimate end. Therefore the Torah promised that when we perform the statutes and commandments, the preparations of this world will come upon us, through which we will merit and live eternal life such as has never reached us before in perfection, neither in the First Temple nor in the Second Temple — this is what is written." "<b>If you walk in My statutes and keep My commandments and do them.</b> The statutes concerning which He warns here are the Sabbatical year and the Jubilee, which He mentioned in the preceding parashah. And He says now that if we walk in them and keep the commandments, He will give the rains in their season and bless for us the grain and the fruit and every plant of the ground, in order that we may have through this abundance and tranquility and all the remaining preparations."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:28:42.685284947Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}